1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 4.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,850
1, Your First Domain Controller.

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:10,500
This is where the pod stops being a group
of separate servers and becomes a domain.

4
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:15,950
You will learn what Active Directory
Domain Services is, with its key words:

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:18,600
domain, tree, forest and domain
controller.

6
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:23,250
You will meet the global catalog, the FSMO
roles and sites.

7
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:28,700
You will see why Active Directory depends
on DNS, choose functional levels, promote

8
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:34,150
DC01 with the wizard and with PowerShell,
and prove the new domain works.

9
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:39,200
Active Directory Domain Services, or AD
DS, is a Windows Server role.

10
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:43,450
It keeps one central list of users,
computers and groups.

11
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:48,500
That list is a directory: a database of
objects you can search.

12
00:00:48,750 --> 00:00:55,150
Each object, like the user Ann Lee or the
computer SRV01, has attributes such as a

13
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:56,600
name.

14
00:00:56,850 --> 00:01:00,850
The schema is the rulebook for which
objects may exist.

15
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:07,500
A domain controller runs AD DS, holds a
copy of the directory and signs people in.

16
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:11,350
For Server+, directory services is one
more server role.

17
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,000
A domain is a group of objects that share
one directory database and one set of

18
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:19,450
policies.

19
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:22,500
It has a DNS-style name, like sa1.

20
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:23,950
lab.

21
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:28,600
A child domain's name ends with its
parent's name, like branch.

22
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:30,050
sa1.

23
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:31,500
lab.

24
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:34,950
Domains in one continuous namespace form a
tree.

25
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,000
One or more trees form a forest.

26
00:01:38,250 --> 00:01:42,250
The forest is the outer security boundary,
with one schema.

27
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:46,500
Global catalog servers hold a partial copy
of every object.

28
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:50,350
The first domain you create is the forest
root.

29
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:53,800
Our pod: one forest, one tree, one domain.

30
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:59,650
A global catalog is a domain controller
that also keeps a searchable summary of

31
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:01,900
every object in the forest.

32
00:02:02,150 --> 00:02:04,550
The first DC is always one.

33
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:10,400
Five special jobs are done by just one DC
each, so changes never clash.

34
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:12,650
These are the FSMO roles.

35
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:16,100
Schema master and domain naming master are
forest-wide.

36
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:20,350
RID master, PDC emulator and
infrastructure master exist once per

37
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:21,800
domain.

38
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:25,250
Sites map Active Directory to your
physical network.

39
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:27,900
A new forest has one site.

40
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:30,550
In our pod, DC01 holds everything.

41
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:34,000
How does a computer find a domain
controller?

42
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:35,450
It asks DNS.

43
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:40,900
It looks up an SRV record named underscore
ldap, underscore tcp, dc, underscore

44
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:42,350
msdcs, sa1.

45
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:43,800
lab.

46
00:02:44,050 --> 00:02:45,650
The answer says: DC01.

47
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:47,100
sa1.

48
00:02:47,350 --> 00:02:48,550
lab, port 389.

49
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:52,000
Then an A record gives the address, 192.

50
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:53,450
168.

51
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:54,900
50.

52
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:56,350
10.

53
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:58,200
No DNS, no domain.

54
00:02:58,450 --> 00:03:02,450
When you promote DC01, the wizard creates
two zones, sa1.

55
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:04,300
lab and underscore msdcs.

56
00:03:04,550 --> 00:03:05,750
sa1.

57
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,200
lab.

58
00:03:07,450 --> 00:03:11,850
Unlike the file-backed zone from Learning
Plan 3, these are Active

59
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:13,300
Directory-integrated.

60
00:03:13,550 --> 00:03:18,750
They live in the directory, replicate with
it, and allow secure dynamic updates.

61
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,200
A functional level decides which Active
Directory features a domain or forest can

62
00:03:24,450 --> 00:03:29,650
use, and so the oldest Windows Server
version allowed as a domain controller.

63
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:35,900
On Windows Server 2025, a new forest must
be at the 2016 level or higher.

64
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:42,150
The Windows Server 2025 level means every
DC must run Windows Server 2025, and it

65
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:44,000
unlocks larger database pages.

66
00:03:44,250 --> 00:03:47,450
The default usually matches the version
you run.

67
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:52,100
In the lab you use Windows Server 2025 for
both levels.

68
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:56,750
You can raise a level later, but not
simply undo it.

69
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,400
Step one is installing the role.

70
00:03:59,650 --> 00:04:03,250
In Server Manager, choose Manage, Add
Roles and Features.

71
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:08,300
On Server Roles, select Active Directory
Domain Services, then select Add Features

72
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:10,550
so the tools come along.

73
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:12,400
Click through and install.

74
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:18,250
Here is the catch: the role alone does not
make DC01 a domain controller.

75
00:04:18,500 --> 00:04:21,300
Server Manager shows a flag: configuration
required.

76
00:04:21,550 --> 00:04:26,750
The link Promote this server to a domain
controller opens the next wizard.

77
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,400
In PowerShell, Install-WindowsFeature
AD-Domain-Services with dash

78
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:31,650
IncludeManagementTools does the same
install.

79
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:35,500
The Active Directory Domain Services
Configuration Wizard starts with

80
00:04:35,750 --> 00:04:36,950
Deployment Configuration.

81
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:42,800
You get three choices: add a domain
controller to an existing domain, add a

82
00:04:43,050 --> 00:04:47,450
new domain to an existing forest, or add a
new forest.

83
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:52,900
DC01 is the very first domain controller,
so choose Add a new forest.

84
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:55,950
Then type the root domain name, sa1.

85
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:57,400
lab.

86
00:04:57,650 --> 00:04:59,650
Use two or more labels.

87
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:03,100
A single-label name like sa1 causes
problems later.

88
00:05:03,350 --> 00:05:07,750
At work, use a subdomain of a name your
organization owns.

89
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,400
On Domain Controller Options, choose
Windows Server 2025 for both functional

90
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:13,850
levels.

91
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:18,500
Keep Domain Name System server selected,
so DC01 hosts the zones.

92
00:05:18,750 --> 00:05:24,750
Global Catalog is selected and greyed out,
because the first DC must be a global

93
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,200
catalog.

94
00:05:26,450 --> 00:05:32,850
Read only domain controller is greyed out
too: an RODC needs a writable DC to copy

95
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:34,300
from.

96
00:05:34,550 --> 00:05:36,950
You build one in Lab 4.

97
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:38,400
4.

98
00:05:38,650 --> 00:05:40,650
Last, type the DSRM password.

99
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:45,700
Directory Services Restore Mode starts
DC01 with AD DS offline, for repairs.

100
00:05:45,950 --> 00:05:49,550
In the pod, use the password shown in
NetLab.

101
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:51,800
Next come three quick pages.

102
00:05:52,050 --> 00:05:57,650
DNS Options shows a yellow message: a
delegation for this DNS server cannot be

103
00:05:57,900 --> 00:05:59,100
created.

104
00:05:59,350 --> 00:06:00,550
That is expected.

105
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:07,200
A delegation lives in the parent zone, and
nobody runs a parent zone for dot lab.

106
00:06:07,450 --> 00:06:09,450
Leave Create DNS delegation cleared.

107
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:12,500
Additional Options shows the NetBIOS
domain name.

108
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:14,750
Wait for SA1 to appear.

109
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,400
That short name is what you type in SA1
backslash Administrator.

110
00:06:19,650 --> 00:06:23,250
Paths shows where the database, logs and
SYSVOL go.

111
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:25,100
Keep all the defaults.

112
00:06:25,350 --> 00:06:28,950
Review Options lists every choice: a new
forest, sa1.

113
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:33,600
lab, NetBIOS SA1, Windows Server 2025
levels, global catalog and DNS.

114
00:06:33,850 --> 00:06:37,850
View script shows the same choices as a
PowerShell command.

115
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:40,100
Then the Prerequisites Check runs.

116
00:06:40,350 --> 00:06:45,550
You will see yellow warnings about an old
cryptography setting and the DNS

117
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:47,000
delegation.

118
00:06:47,250 --> 00:06:51,250
Yellow is fine here; a red error would
stop you.

119
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:54,300
When all prerequisite checks pass, select
Install.

120
00:06:54,550 --> 00:06:57,750
When it is done, DC01 restarts by itself.

121
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,800
The first start takes a few minutes.

122
00:07:01,050 --> 00:07:03,050
Here is the PowerShell way.

123
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:06,900
After installing the role, run
Install-ADDSForest with dash DomainName

124
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:08,350
sa1.

125
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:13,000
lab, dash DomainNetbiosName SA1, dash
InstallDns, and dash ForestMode and dash

126
00:07:13,250 --> 00:07:16,450
DomainMode set to Win2025, as in the
wizard.

127
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:22,300
The cmdlet asks twice for the safe mode
administrator password, which is the DSRM

128
00:07:22,550 --> 00:07:24,150
password, and hides it.

129
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:27,600
Never put that password in a script file.

130
00:07:27,850 --> 00:07:31,450
Then you confirm, and the server restarts
when done.

131
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:36,500
Below is the long form from View script,
with the default paths.

132
00:07:36,750 --> 00:07:40,350
After the restart, sign in as SA1
backslash Administrator.

133
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:47,000
The password is the same, because on the
first DC of a new forest the local

134
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:49,250
Administrator becomes the domain
Administrator.

135
00:07:49,500 --> 00:07:52,700
A domain controller has no local user
accounts.

136
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:54,550
Run whoami to confirm.

137
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:57,200
Then look at ipconfig slash all.

138
00:07:57,450 --> 00:08:03,050
The DNS server list now starts with the
loopback addresses, colon colon one and

139
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:04,500
127.

140
00:08:04,750 --> 00:08:05,950
0.

141
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:07,400
0.

142
00:08:07,650 --> 00:08:08,850
1.

143
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:12,300
DC01 asks itself, because it hosts the
zones.

144
00:08:12,550 --> 00:08:16,550
And the Tools menu now lists the Active
Directory consoles.

145
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:18,400
Now prove it worked.

146
00:08:18,650 --> 00:08:21,450
Get-ADDomain, piped to Format-List, shows
DNSRoot sa1.

147
00:08:21,700 --> 00:08:24,900
lab and NetBIOSName SA1, the names you
typed.

148
00:08:25,150 --> 00:08:30,350
DistinguishedName is DC equals sa1, DC
equals lab, the way the directory writes

149
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:31,800
the domain name.

150
00:08:32,050 --> 00:08:34,850
DomainMode shows the Windows Server 2025
level.

151
00:08:35,100 --> 00:08:38,700
The three domain-wide FSMO roles all point
at DC01.

152
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:40,150
sa1.

153
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:41,600
lab.

154
00:08:41,850 --> 00:08:46,250
Get-ADForest shows the two forest-wide
roles, schema master and domain naming

155
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:48,100
master, also on DC01.

156
00:08:48,350 --> 00:08:52,350
GlobalCatalogs lists DC01, and Sites shows
the single default site.

157
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:54,600
One server holds every role.

158
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:56,050
Next, DNS.

159
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:58,300
Open DNS Manager on DC01.

160
00:08:58,550 --> 00:09:01,750
Under Forward Lookup Zones you see
underscore msdcs.

161
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:03,200
sa1.

162
00:09:03,450 --> 00:09:04,650
lab and sa1.

163
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:06,100
lab.

164
00:09:06,350 --> 00:09:07,550
Inside sa1.

165
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:12,600
lab, the underscore tcp folder holds SRV
records for LDAP and Kerberos.

166
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:18,450
Now test the lookup clients use: nslookup
dash type equals SRV, then the long

167
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:19,900
underscore ldap name.

168
00:09:20,150 --> 00:09:21,750
The answer is DC01.

169
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,200
sa1.

170
00:09:23,450 --> 00:09:26,250
lab on port 389, plus its address.

171
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:28,500
Last, run dcdiag slash q.

172
00:09:28,750 --> 00:09:33,150
Quiet mode prints only errors, so no
output is good news.

173
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:36,200
After a restart, wait a few minutes.

174
00:09:36,450 --> 00:09:38,050
Four mistakes to avoid.

175
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:42,300
One: clearing the DNS server box on the
first DC.

176
00:09:42,550 --> 00:09:47,350
Without DNS there are no SRV records, and
nobody finds the domain.

177
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:52,800
Two: a single-label domain name, or a
public name you do not own.

178
00:09:53,050 --> 00:09:54,250
Use sa1.

179
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:59,700
lab in the lab, and a subdomain of your
own name at work.

180
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:04,350
Three: pointing DC01's DNS client at SRV01
or a public server.

181
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:06,200
A DC asks itself.

182
00:10:06,450 --> 00:10:09,650
Four: treating the DSRM password as a
throwaway.

183
00:10:09,900 --> 00:10:14,300
It is your way into restore mode, so keep
it safe.

184
00:10:14,550 --> 00:10:16,150
Time to check yourself.

185
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:20,800
DC01 is now the first and only domain
controller of sa1.

186
00:10:21,050 --> 00:10:22,250
lab.

187
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:28,100
First: which DNS record type tells SRV01
where the domain controller is, and what

188
00:10:28,350 --> 00:10:29,550
does it hold?

189
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:34,600
Second: why was Read only domain
controller greyed out on Domain Controller

190
00:10:34,850 --> 00:10:36,050
Options?

191
00:10:36,300 --> 00:10:41,100
Third: after the restart, which DNS server
does DC01 use, and why?

192
00:10:41,350 --> 00:10:46,550
Fourth: which two FSMO roles are
forest-wide, and which DC holds all five

193
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:48,400
roles in the pod?

194
00:10:48,650 --> 00:10:51,850
Pause the video now and answer each one.

195
00:10:52,100 --> 00:10:53,700
Here are the answers.

196
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:55,550
One: an SRV record.

197
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:59,800
The name underscore ldap, underscore tcp,
dc, underscore msdcs, sa1.

198
00:11:00,050 --> 00:11:01,650
lab points to DC01.

199
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:03,100
sa1.

200
00:11:03,350 --> 00:11:07,750
lab on port 389, and an A record then
gives 192.

201
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:09,200
168.

202
00:11:09,450 --> 00:11:10,650
50.

203
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:12,100
10.

204
00:11:12,350 --> 00:11:15,150
Two: the first DC must be writable.

205
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:19,800
An RODC copies from a writable DC, and
none exists yet.

206
00:11:20,050 --> 00:11:24,450
Three: DC01 uses itself, through loopback,
because it hosts the Active

207
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:25,900
Directory-integrated zones.

208
00:11:26,150 --> 00:11:29,750
Four: schema master and domain naming
master are forest-wide.

209
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,400
The other three are per domain, and DC01
holds all five.

210
00:11:34,650 --> 00:11:36,650
Let's do it on DC01.

211
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:41,300
It is still in a workgroup, with the
static address 192.

212
00:11:41,550 --> 00:11:42,750
168.

213
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,200
50.

214
00:11:44,450 --> 00:11:45,650
10.

215
00:11:45,900 --> 00:11:52,700
In Server Manager I add the AD DS role
with its tools, then use the flag to

216
00:11:52,950 --> 00:11:54,150
promote the server.

217
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:57,200
I choose Add a new forest, sa1.

218
00:11:57,450 --> 00:12:03,450
lab, keep Windows Server 2025 and DNS, and
type the DSRM password, which is the

219
00:12:03,700 --> 00:12:06,100
password shown in your NetLab pod.

220
00:12:06,350 --> 00:12:11,950
I accept the delegation warning, check
SA1, keep the paths, and peek at View

221
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:13,400
script.

222
00:12:13,650 --> 00:12:14,850
Install.

223
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:20,300
After the restart I sign in as SA1
backslash Administrator and run each

224
00:12:20,550 --> 00:12:21,750
check.

225
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:23,200
To wrap up.

226
00:12:23,450 --> 00:12:27,850
AD DS is a directory of objects, held by
domain controllers.

227
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:33,300
Domains group objects, trees share a
namespace, and the forest is the security

228
00:12:33,550 --> 00:12:34,750
boundary.

229
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:40,600
Clients find domain controllers through
SRV records in DNS, so the DNS server box

230
00:12:40,850 --> 00:12:42,050
matters.

231
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:48,300
To promote the first DC, choose Add a new
forest, Windows Server 2025 levels, DNS

232
00:12:48,550 --> 00:12:50,550
server, and a DSRM password.

233
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:54,400
Then verify with Get-ADDomain,
Get-ADForest, DNS Manager, nslookup and

234
00:12:54,650 --> 00:12:55,850
dcdiag.

235
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:57,300
Lab 4.

236
00:12:57,550 --> 00:13:01,150
1 practices all of this in about fifty
minutes.
